WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.387
Welcome to the explainer.

2
00:00:01.387 --> 00:00:08.713
So, you know when you look up at a massive skyscraper and wonder about the blueprint that keeps it standing strong against the wind,

3
00:00:08.713 --> 00:00:10.544
the rain, and just sheer gravity?

4
00:00:10.544 --> 00:00:14.873
Well, your financial life requires a blueprint that's honestly just as robust.

5
00:00:14.873 --> 00:00:20.312
Today, we're building a foundational understanding of risk and insurance right from the ground up.

6
00:00:20.312 --> 00:00:25.085
We're going to explore exactly how to architect a plan that protects you, your family,

7
00:00:25.085 --> 00:00:28.414
and your assets from all those unpredictable forces of life.

8
00:00:28.414 --> 00:00:29.913
Okay, let's dive into this.

9
00:00:29.913 --> 00:00:36.739
Our syllabus today is broken down into six parts: one, decoding risks and hazards; two, the risk management process; three,

10
00:00:36.739 --> 00:00:44.065
the law of insurance contracts; four, navigating torts and liability; five, the underwriting and claims process; and six, producers,

11
00:00:44.065 --> 00:00:45.341
regulation and ratings.

12
00:00:45.341 --> 00:00:48.005
All right, part one, decoding risks and hazards.

13
00:00:48.005 --> 00:00:51.446
This is where we establish the raw materials of our blueprint.

14
00:00:51.446 --> 00:00:57.273
To start things off, we've really got to separate three distinct concepts: a risk, a peril, and a hazard.

15
00:00:57.273 --> 00:01:02.545
A risk is simply the possibility of a loss, like the possibility of your house getting damaged.

16
00:01:02.545 --> 00:01:06.652
A peril, on the other hand, is the actual cause of that loss, like a fire.

17
00:01:06.652 --> 00:01:12.923
But a hazard, that's a condition that actively increases the potential for the loss to happen in the first place.

18
00:01:12.923 --> 00:01:18.695
Imagine a pile of oily shop rags sitting in the corner of a garage right next to a glowing space heater.

19
00:01:18.695 --> 00:01:22.913
The fire is the peril, sure, but those oily rags, that is a textbook hazard.

20
00:01:22.913 --> 00:01:27.963
And as financial planners, our job is literally to spot those rags before they ever ignite.

21
00:01:27.963 --> 00:01:31.182
Now, insurance companies won't just cover anything, right?

22
00:01:31.182 --> 00:01:34.067
They strictly categorize risk into two main buckets.

23
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:38.174
The first is pure risk, which involves only the chance of loss or no loss.

24
00:01:38.174 --> 00:01:40.006
Think of your house burning down.

25
00:01:40.006 --> 00:01:41.948
There's absolutely no upside there.

26
00:01:41.948 --> 00:01:43.557
No chance of gain, just loss.

27
00:01:43.557 --> 00:01:46.277
These are the risks insurers will actually cover.

28
00:01:46.277 --> 00:01:51.271
Contrast that with speculative risk, which involves a chance of loss and a chance of gain.

29
00:01:51.271 --> 00:01:55.545
If you take your money to a casino to gamble, you might lose it all, but hey,

30
00:01:55.545 --> 00:01:59.041
you might hit the jackpot because there is that chance of gain.

31
00:01:59.041 --> 00:02:01.427
Speculative risks are strictly uninsurable.

32
00:02:01.427 --> 00:02:04.924
Moving right along to section two, the risk management process.

33
00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:08.698
This is all about actively managing the risks we've just identified.

34
00:02:08.698 --> 00:02:12.305
There is a highly structured six-step process to risk management.

35
00:02:12.305 --> 00:02:17.966
You start out by identifying your tolerance for loss and then gathering every piece of pertinent data.

36
00:02:17.966 --> 00:02:20.907
Everything from home inventories to medical policies.

37
00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:28.621
After that, you analyze the data, develop a treatment plan, communicate those recommendations, and finally, you implement and monitor them.

38
00:02:28.621 --> 00:02:31.784
But you want to know the real secret to making this work?

39
00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:35.225
Client involvement is absolutely crucial at every single step.

40
00:02:35.225 --> 00:02:38.832
If you're building a plan with someone rather than just for them,

41
00:02:38.832 --> 00:02:44.160
there are no unhappy surprises when it's time to actually buy the insurance or make the changes.

42
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.156
You know, you hear a ton about saving 15% of your income for retirement.

43
00:02:48.156 --> 00:02:53.539
But get this, families on average spend a minimum of 10% of their income just on risk protection.

44
00:02:53.539 --> 00:03:00.865
Between health, auto, home, and life insurance, a truly massive chunk of our financial lives is dedicated solely to mitigating risk.

45
00:03:00.865 --> 00:03:05.748
And this brilliantly illustrates how we decide what to actually do with a specific risk.

46
00:03:05.748 --> 00:03:11.687
You basically measure it by frequency, so, how often it happens, and severity, how much it's going to cost.

47
00:03:11.687 --> 00:03:18.013
If a risk is low frequency but really high severity, like a house fire, we transfer that risk by buying insurance.

48
00:03:18.013 --> 00:03:23.729
But what if a risk is low frequency and low severity, like an old cheap car getting a little door ding?

49
00:03:23.729 --> 00:03:24.451
We retain it.

50
00:03:24.451 --> 00:03:25.894
We just pay out of pocket.

51
00:03:25.894 --> 00:03:30.556
It is a really simple, logical grid, but it drives some massive financial decisions.

52
00:03:30.556 --> 00:03:30.833
Okay.

53
00:03:30.833 --> 00:03:33.219
Part three, the law of insurance contracts.

54
00:03:33.219 --> 00:03:36.105
Let's look at formalizing the transfer of that risk.

55
00:03:36.105 --> 00:03:41.267
For any insurance policy to be legally binding, it absolutely must meet five strict criteria.

56
00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:43.875
First, there has to be an offer and acceptance.

57
00:03:43.875 --> 00:03:49.369
Second, there must be consideration, which just means you pay a premium and they bind the coverage.

58
00:03:49.369 --> 00:03:52.033
Third, the object of the contract must be legal.

59
00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:55.585
You obviously cannot insure an illegal counterfeiting operation.

60
00:03:55.585 --> 00:03:58.304
Fourth, the parties have to be legally competent.

61
00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:01.745
And fifth, the contract itself must be in a proper legal form.

62
00:04:01.745 --> 00:04:06.351
Literally, if even one of these pillars is missing, the whole contract falls apart.

63
00:04:06.351 --> 00:04:09.959
Insurance contracts also have some really unique characteristics.

64
00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:12.400
They're what's called contracts of adhesion.

65
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.341
Basically, this means the insurance company writes the entire contract.

66
00:04:16.341 --> 00:04:22.168
You, the buyer, have absolutely no power to grab a pen, cross out a paragraph, and negotiate the wording.

67
00:04:22.168 --> 00:04:24.166
It's completely take it or leave it.

68
00:04:24.166 --> 00:04:26.164
On top of that, they're conditional.

69
00:04:26.164 --> 00:04:31.713
The insurer is only going to pay if you meet the specific conditions outlined in that very contract,

70
00:04:31.713 --> 00:04:35.376
like paying your premiums on time and reporting any loss promptly.

71
00:04:35.376 --> 00:04:40.315
And the absolute golden rule governing all these contracts is the principle of indemnity.

72
00:04:40.315 --> 00:04:44.811
The entire purpose of insurance is to make you whole again, not to make you rich.

73
00:04:44.811 --> 00:04:50.194
So, if you have a $5, 000 fender bender, the insurance company pays to fix the $5, 000 in damage,

74
00:04:50.194 --> 00:04:55.300
putting you back in the exact financial position you were in just a moment before the crash.

75
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.297
You don't get to profit from a loss.

76
00:04:57.297 --> 00:04:59.795
Section four, navigating torts and liability.

77
00:04:59.795 --> 00:05:02.403
Let's explore legal liabilities and negligence.

78
00:05:02.403 --> 00:05:08.841
When someone unintentionally causes physical or financial harm to someone else, it usually boils down to negligence.

79
00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:13.558
And to actually prove negligence in court, four distinct elements have to be present.

80
00:05:13.558 --> 00:05:16.832
First, a duty is owed, like the basic duty to drive safely.

81
00:05:16.832 --> 00:05:18.497
Second, the duty was breached.

82
00:05:18.497 --> 00:05:19.996
Say you rear-ended someone.

83
00:05:19.996 --> 00:05:21.883
Third, actual damage has occurred.

84
00:05:21.883 --> 00:05:23.659
Their bumper is totally crushed.

85
00:05:23.659 --> 00:05:25.712
And fourth, there is proximate cause.

86
00:05:25.712 --> 00:05:29.874
You were texting, which caused you to not see them, which caused the crash.

87
00:05:29.874 --> 00:05:32.316
All four of these things must link together.

88
00:05:32.316 --> 00:05:35.646
But wait, what if you weren't even the one doing the acting?

89
00:05:35.646 --> 00:05:37.089
Enter vicarious liability.

90
00:05:37.089 --> 00:05:41.251
This is when you are held legally responsible for the acts of someone else.

91
00:05:41.251 --> 00:05:48.854
So, if your kid is playing out in the yard and accidentally hits a baseball straight through the neighbor's expensive living room window,

92
00:05:48.854 --> 00:05:51.352
you, as the parent, have vicarious liability.

93
00:05:51.352 --> 00:05:57.623
The exact same concept applies to an employer whose employee causes a major accident while out making a delivery.

94
00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:02.562
Historically, courts handled blame using something called strict contributory negligence.

95
00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:09.943
Under this rule, if you are even 1% at fault for an accident, like say you were speeding slightly when someone blatantly cut you off,

96
00:06:09.943 --> 00:06:12.773
you could recover absolutely zero damages, nothing.

97
00:06:12.773 --> 00:06:13.994
It's incredibly harsh.

98
00:06:13.994 --> 00:06:19.655
Because of that, today most states use comparative negligence, which is a much more standard approach.

99
00:06:19.655 --> 00:06:25.760
Here, if a jury decides you were, say, 20% to blame, your final financial award is simply reduced by that 20%.

100
00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.314
Much more reasonable, right?

101
00:06:27.314 --> 00:06:27.869
All right.

102
00:06:27.869 --> 00:06:30.643
Section five, the underwriting and claims process.

103
00:06:30.643 --> 00:06:34.084
This is where we look at evaluating risks and settling losses.

104
00:06:34.084 --> 00:06:41.188
The folks evaluating your application are called underwriters, and they are deeply concerned with two specific types of hazards.

105
00:06:41.188 --> 00:06:44.074
The first is a moral hazard, with no "e" at the end.

106
00:06:44.074 --> 00:06:49.623
This is outright unethical behavior like lying on your application to hide a past DUI or even worse,

107
00:06:49.623 --> 00:06:52.953
burning down your own building just for the insurance money.

108
00:06:52.953 --> 00:06:55.340
The second is a morale hazard, with an "e".

109
00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:59.058
So, the crucial point is that morale hazard isn't malicious at all.

110
00:06:59.058 --> 00:07:00.445
It's simply indifference.

111
00:07:00.445 --> 00:07:05.107
It's leaving your keys in an unlocked car just because you figure, eh, it's insured.

112
00:07:05.107 --> 00:07:08.825
Let me tell you, underwriters are always looking for signs of both.

113
00:07:08.825 --> 00:07:11.378
So, why are underwriters so incredibly strict?

114
00:07:11.378 --> 00:07:14.542
Well, it's because of a concept called adverse selection.

115
00:07:14.542 --> 00:07:20.313
This is the reality that people who expect to have losses are the very ones who want insurance the most.

116
00:07:20.313 --> 00:07:26.029
I mean, if you have a terrible driving record, you are highly motivated to buy top-tier auto insurance.

117
00:07:26.029 --> 00:07:29.470
To combat this, insurance companies use gatekeeper mechanisms.

118
00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:37.739
They implement deductibles and strict underwriting guidelines just to ensure they don't end up with a pool entirely made up of high-risk individuals.

119
00:07:37.739 --> 00:07:41.402
Because if they did, it would literally bankrupt the whole system.

120
00:07:41.402 --> 00:07:44.898
But what happens when a loss actually occurs in the real world?

121
00:07:44.898 --> 00:07:51.281
A claims adjuster takes over and follows a very strict three-step loss adjustment process on behalf of the insurer.

122
00:07:51.281 --> 00:07:52.723
First up is investigation.

123
00:07:52.723 --> 00:07:55.831
They evaluate all the relevant information and evidence.

124
00:07:55.831 --> 00:07:57.163
Second is proof of loss.

125
00:07:57.163 --> 00:08:00.715
They require the insured to officially document all the damages.

126
00:08:00.715 --> 00:08:02.435
And third is payment or denial.

127
00:08:02.435 --> 00:08:05.599
Based on the conditions in that contract we talked about,

128
00:08:05.599 --> 00:08:10.261
they either issue the check to indemnify the insured or they legally deny the claim.

129
00:08:10.261 --> 00:08:13.590
And finally, section six, producers, regulation and ratings.

130
00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:16.976
Let's talk about the builders and regulators of the industry.

131
00:08:16.976 --> 00:08:21.526
When you're out there buying insurance, you're usually working through a producer,

132
00:08:21.526 --> 00:08:26.077
but the legal distinction of who that producer actually represents is super vital.

133
00:08:26.077 --> 00:08:28.408
An agent represents the insurance company.

134
00:08:28.408 --> 00:08:33.181
By law, an agent has the apparent authority to legally bind the insurer to a contract.

135
00:08:33.181 --> 00:08:37.843
So, if an agent says you have coverage, the company is generally on the hook for it.

136
00:08:37.843 --> 00:08:40.340
A broker, however, represents you, the buyer.

137
00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:47.222
They can shop around for the best rates for you, but they absolutely cannot legally bind an insurance company to a contract.

138
00:08:47.222 --> 00:08:53.326
Now, how on earth do you know the insurance company you're buying from won't just go out of business tomorrow?

139
00:08:53.326 --> 00:09:00.041
The industry is heavily regulated at the state level by the National Association of Insurance Commissioners, or the NAIC.

140
00:09:00.041 --> 00:09:04.481
But for true financial strength, we have to look to independent rating agencies.

141
00:09:04.481 --> 00:09:10.086
While companies like Moody's and Standard and Poor's provide ratings, AM Best really stands out here.

142
00:09:10.086 --> 00:09:16.579
It is the oldest rating service that uniquely specializes in analyzing the financial strength of insurance companies.

143
00:09:16.579 --> 00:09:21.297
Checking a company's AM Best rating is a totally crucial final step in our blueprint.

144
00:09:21.297 --> 00:09:25.015
We have covered a massive amount of ground in this explainer today.

145
00:09:25.015 --> 00:09:30.842
From the basic definitions of hazards all the way up to the complex realities of contracts and liability.

146
00:09:30.842 --> 00:09:34.782
You now understand the underlying architecture of real risk management.

147
00:09:34.782 --> 00:09:42.497
So, I'll leave you with this final thought to consider: Looking at your own life right now, what risks are you unknowingly retaining today?

148
00:09:42.497 --> 00:09:45.826
And just how solid is your financial blueprint against them?

149
00:09:45.826 --> 00:09:49.989
Take a look at your policies, ask those hard questions, and start building.

150
00:09:49.989 --> 00:09:52.597
Thanks so much for joining me on the explainer.

